---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../config";

const aboutUrl = new URL("/about/", SITE.url).href;
const authorUrl = new URL(SITE.author.url, SITE.url).href;

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  url: aboutUrl,
  mainEntity: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: SITE.name,
    url: SITE.url,
    description: SITE.description,
    founder: { "@type": "Person", name: SITE.author.name, url: authorUrl },
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`${SITE.name} is an independent site of crochet tutorials, charts, and beginner-friendly patterns, published by ${SITE.author.name}.`}
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-10 sm:px-8 sm:py-14">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
      About {SITE.name}
    </h1>

    <div class="prose mt-8">
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is a place to learn crochet in plain US English. Start with
        what to buy and the basic stitches, then move on to small projects you
        can actually finish: a scarf, a hat, a bag, a holiday piece.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={SITE.author.url}>{SITE.author.name}</a> publishes and edits
        the site, with a focus on practical patterns and beginner-friendly
        lessons. {SITE.author.name} is not a crochet designer and does not hold
        a teaching credential.
      </p>

      <h2>What you will find</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/start-here/">12-step course</a> is the path through the
        basics. The <a href="/charts/">charts</a> are there when you need a
        hook size or a yarn weight. The
        <a href="/tutorials/">tutorials</a> explain one technique at a time, and
        the <a href="/free-patterns/">patterns</a> are small projects that use
        those skills. Where a UK term names a different stitch, the page says
        so.
      </p>

      <h2>Sizes, pictures, and what they mean</h2>
      <p>
        A size on a pattern page is worked out from the gauge written on that
        page. It is not a measurement taken from a finished sample, and the
        yardage is an estimate. Pictures of finished objects are illustrations
        of the intended look. They are not photographs of a project this site
        has made. The <a href="/disclosures/">disclosures page</a> explains
        that in one place.
      </p>

      <h2>If something looks wrong</h2>
      <p>
        Write through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> and name the
        page and the row. A real mistake gets fixed, and the article's updated
        date changes with it.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site will not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Republish another designer's pattern. Patterns are their authors'
          work. When a pattern is featured here, the link goes to the designer's
          own page so they get the visit.
        </li>
        <li>
          Present an illustration as a photograph of something that was made.
        </li>
        <li>
          Recommend a product for a commission. The
          <a href="/disclosures/">disclosures page</a> explains how the gear
          guides work.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How the site pays for itself</h2>
      <p>
        Display advertising and affiliate links in the hooks and yarn guides.
        Both are explained on the <a href="/disclosures/">disclosures page</a>,
        and neither costs you anything. Every tutorial and chart is free.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
